            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a breastplate, then the Latin for a round shield."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: obsideō, obsidēre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "obsideō, obsidēre — \"to besiege; to sit at\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hostēs oppidum trēs mēnsēs obsēdērunt. The enemy besieged the town for three months."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "obsideō, obsidēre, obsēdī, obsessum: ob + sedeō (taught), to sit down in front of. English obsess."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "obsideō is built on sedeō, \"to sit\", with ob, \"in front of\" (Lewis & Short): to sit down before a town. English obsess comes from its participle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
